# .publication-tokens — example
#
# Copy this file to `.publication-tokens` (same directory) and list the
# environment-specific vocabulary that must NEVER appear in a shipped file:
# organisation names, internal codenames, host prefixes, product names,
# usernames — anything specific to YOUR setup rather than to superhuman.
#
#   cp .publication-tokens.example .publication-tokens
#   # then edit .publication-tokens to list your own tokens
#
# The real `.publication-tokens` is gitignored, so your vocabulary never ships.
# This `.example` file is tracked only to document the format.
#
# Format:
#   - one token per line
#   - blank lines and lines beginning with `#` are ignored
#   - matching is case-insensitive substring matching
#
# The publication guard (tests/test_content.py :: test_operator_tokens_are_absent)
# reads `.publication-tokens` and fails the suite if any listed token appears in a
# tracked file. When `.publication-tokens` is absent the check skips silently, so
# seeding this list is opt-in — but doing so is what keeps a fork's own internal
# vocabulary out of the published tree.
#
# Example tokens (delete these and add your own):
# acme-corp
# project-nightingale
# prod-gateway
# jdoe
